            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a fair amount of air leakage.



I noted a significant amount of air leakage through the fireplace in the living room. As you are already planning on removing or repairing this fireplace I recommend that you prioritize this upgrade.



I noted a significant amount of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 

 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors, in particular your patio doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



If you are replacing any of your bathroom fans I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. 



Other upgrade recommendations in this report may also affect the air leakage rate. 



Savings: For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially save $120 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ, and qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



There is also a small exposed floor in the front of your house. This section is too small to qualify for a rebate, but I recommend that when you are upgrading your exterior walls, you also upgrade the insulation in this floor. 



Savings:

By increasing your walls by a minimum R15, you could potentially save $650 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

Since I was not able to access the attic, the attic of your house was assumed to have 8 inches (R24) of blown fiberglass insulation.  I have modelled adding blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Your cathedral ceiling savings are calculated separately.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.2, and qualify for up to $367.50 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).

As you mentioned you may potentially reuse the inulation from the wall cavities in attic, the re-used insulation would not qualify under the rebate, however if any additional insulation is purchased for insulating the attic, this would qualify. 





To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house was assumed to have 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R24) rigid foam, polyisocyanurate insulation.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R44, you could potentially save $210 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2, and qualify for up to $255.00 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls in the boiler room appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 6 inches (R20)fibreglass batt insulation to this section.



On the left side of your house, the crawlspace appears to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation, and the basement walls in the living space appears to have 2x4 framing with R12 insulation. I have modelled the potential savings of bringing the insulation in this space up to R20, and adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam insulation to the floor of the crawlspace. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 walls with R20 fibreglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding the same 3 inches (R15) of exterior insulation to the pony walls of your basement. 



Should you add insulation to the floor of your crawlspace, you may wish to consult with a contractor to ensure that any moisture that collects in the space will be allowed to dry out. 



Savings:

By adding insulation to your basement as described above, you could potentially save $1460 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 40.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $842.50 in rebates from the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. 



To be eligible for a basement insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

Some of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. However, they are all in reasonable shape. Since you are considering an exterior wall insulation retrofit, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the double pane windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the double pane windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $150 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STARÂ® qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 178 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,090 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Alternately, you could heat your water using your current boiler with a combi loop. As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop, and incorporate an insulated storage tank to prevent the boiler from "short cycling".



Savings:

By using your boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $860 per year in energy costs, but increase your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ.  

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This allows you to eliminate both the chimney and the combustion air into your basement.



For this upgrade I have also eliminated the fireplace in your living room. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $1,960 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 42.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



As you have indicated that you would like to replace your basement pellet stove with a newer model, I recommend you install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.



Savings:

By a Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $110 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



